WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.920
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:04.890
DUSTIN TINGLEY: How can you
be ready for what's ahead?

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:08.130
Data science has gone
from hype to reality.

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:11.070
What was once the realm of
scientists and engineers

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:15.720
has become central to health care,
government, finance, advertising, even

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:17.250
education.

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:20.280
If you're a leader or a
decision maker, or you're just

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:25.270
looking to make sense of data
science, this is your place to start.

00:00:25.270 --> 00:00:27.330
It's not a programming course.

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:29.220
Instead, it's meant
to give you the tools

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:33.690
you need for a world that will never
stop giving you more information.

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:38.760
Featuring real world case studies
and interviews with industry experts.

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:43.350
Digging deep into examples from
health care privacy and more.

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:44.527
We'll show you what worked.

00:00:44.527 --> 00:00:45.360
KADE CROCKFORD: Hey.

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:47.068
I saw what you guys
did in San Francisco.

00:00:47.068 --> 00:00:48.360
Let's do it in Somerville too.

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:50.652
ED DAVIS: Inequities in the
health and criminal justice

00:00:50.652 --> 00:00:55.830
system, police brutality issues, issues
around COVID, these are all problems

00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:57.670
that big data can affect.

00:00:57.670 --> 00:00:59.670
DUSTIN TINGLEY: We'll
show you what didn't work.

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:02.530
CRAIG A. UMSCHEID: Unfortunately,
I think as an institution,

00:01:02.530 --> 00:01:04.035
we got out ahead of ourselves.

00:01:04.035 --> 00:01:05.910
LATANYA SWEENEY: If
somebody takes a data set

00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:10.020
that's supposed to be anonymous and
they re-identify the people in it,

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:11.970
all kinds of harms can happen.

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:14.560
KADE CROCKFORD: This technology
was not just science fiction.

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:16.590
It was becoming science reality.

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:18.480
DUSTIN TINGLEY: And you'll find out why.

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:22.260
The information is at your fingertips.

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:24.360
Are you data science ready?

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:28.010
[MUSIC PLAYING]

